Client PC / Jump Server -> RDP Destination Server: Confirmed & Corrected Path

Confirmed against official CyberArk documentation (docs.cyberark.com), given that CPM and PSM are co-installed
on the same Connector servers (OT-CARK-PAM1 and OT-CARK-PAM2), as you confirmed.

Your proposed path (from the diagram you sent) is CORRECT at a high level:
  (1) PC/Jump Server => Internet => (2) CyberArk PAM (LB) => (3) CyberArk PAM (PSM: OT-CARK-PAM1, OT-CARK-PAM2) => (4) RDP destination server

Expanded, with the step that precedes it and the protocol/port confirmed for each hop:

Step 0 - Authenticate & obtain the RDP connection file (browser, before the RDP session starts):
   PC/Jump Server --HTTPS/443--> olympus.cyberark.cloud (Privilege Cloud portal / PVWA)
   PC/Jump Server --HTTPS/443--> abn4186.id.cyberark.cloud (CyberArk Identity)
   PC/Jump Server --HTTPS/443--> login.microsoftonline.com (Microsoft Entra ID federation)

Hop (1) -> (2): PC/Jump Server --RDP/TCP 3389--> CyberArk PAM LB (otcyberarkilb.is.olympus.global)
   CORRECTED: your template listed this hop as HTTPS/TCP-443. CyberArk's own PSM architecture documentation
   states: 'By default, the user connects to the PSM machine through port 3389, using the RDP protocol... In
   an environment with load balanced PSMs, specify the address of the PSM load balancer.' The HTTPS/443
   traffic in Step 0 is portal login only - the RDP session itself is RDP/3389, not HTTPS.

Hop (2) -> (3): CyberArk PAM LB --RDP/TCP 3389--> OT-CARK-PAM1 (10.98.10.4) and/or OT-CARK-PAM2 (10.98.10.5)
   This hop was missing from the template and has been added - the LB load-balances the RDP session to
   whichever PSM node is selected.

Hop (3) -> (4): Connector (OT-CARK-PAM1 / OT-CARK-PAM2, running PSM) -> RDP destination server
   RDP/TCP 3389 if the target is Windows; SSH/TCP 22 if the target is Linux.
   Per CyberArk's Connector network requirements: 'Connector: TCP/3389 or TCP/22' and 'PSM for SSH: TCP/22'.
   (PSM always receives the client connection as RDP; if the ultimate target is Linux, PSM itself opens the
   outbound SSH session to the target - this is why hops 1-3 are RDP throughout but hop 3-4 can be SSH.)

Firewalls sit between (1)-(2) and (3)-(4) per your note - the ACL pairs to configure are:
   ACL 1: PC/Jump Server <-> CyberArk PAM LB, RDP/TCP 3389
   ACL 2: OT-CARK-PAM1 & OT-CARK-PAM2 <-> RDP destination server(s), RDP/TCP 3389 (Windows) or SSH/TCP 22 (Linux)

Since CPM and PSM sit on the same OT-CARK-PAM1/2 servers, that pair of firewall rules is NOT the whole story -
the same servers also need two additional, separate sets of outbound rules (see the 'Network Communication'
tab, rows 9-24):

  1) Connector <-> CyberArk Privilege Cloud backend (cloud side), required for both CPM and PSM to function:
     - HTTPS/TCP 443 to *.cyberark.cloud - REST/API calls and Connector Management
     - TCP 1858 to *.cyberark.cloud - THIS WAS MISSING FROM YOUR TEMPLATE. It is the primary Vault channel
       (credential retrieval, session data, heartbeat) and is required in addition to port 443.
     - HTTPS/TCP 443 to *.id.cyberark.cloud - Identity/Connector Management authentication
     - HTTP/TCP 80 to *.amazontrust.com - AWS SSL certificate validation only (OCSP), no application data
     CyberArk recommends wildcard (*.cyberark.cloud) rules for these since exact IPs can change; if your
     policy needs static entries, request the exact subdomains/IPs from CyberArk Support.

  2) CPM (password rotation) <-> every managed target, corrected from the port list you referenced:
     - Windows (local, desktop, or domain accounts): Direct Host SMB, TCP/445 only
       (the 139 TCP/UDP, 135 TCP/UDP, and 49152-65535 dynamic RPC range in your reference sheet are from an
       older/legacy port table and are NOT required per current official CyberArk documentation)
     - Domain accounts additionally need: Kerberos TCP+UDP/88, DNS TCP+UDP/53
     - Linux/UNIX: SSH TCP/22 (Telnet TCP/23 only if a target still uses Telnet instead of SSH)

One item NOT in your diagram, worth confirming with him if in use: browser-based (HTML5 Gateway) sessions.
CyberArk also supports launching sessions from the portal in-browser via an SIA server, without downloading
an RDP file (User -> Portal HTTPS/443 -> SIA Server -> LB 3389 -> PSM 3389 -> Target). If this method is also
permitted for his users, it needs its own firewall rule set; it is not required if only the RDP-file method
(as in his diagram) is used.
